# Architecture Rules

Rules for a senior developer persona operating across Go and Python services.

---

## Go — Clean Architecture

### Layer Structure

Every service is divided into exactly three layers, with dependencies pointing inward only:

```
cmd/              ← entry points (gRPC server, REST gateway, CLI)
internal/
  domain/         ← entities, value objects, domain errors (no external deps)
  usecase/        ← business logic, orchestration, interfaces for ports
  infra/          ← data logic: DB repos, external clients, gRPC implementations
proto/            ← .proto definitions (source of truth for API contracts)
```

**Rules:**
- `domain/` imports nothing from `usecase/` or `infra/`.
- `usecase/` defines interfaces (ports); `infra/` implements them.
- `infra/` is the only layer allowed to import DB drivers, HTTP clients, or third-party SDKs.
- No logic lives in `cmd/` — only wiring (DI, config loading, server start).

### gRPC + REST Gateway

- All external-facing APIs are defined as gRPC services in `proto/*.proto`.
- REST endpoints are generated via [grpc-gateway](https://github.com/grpc-ecosystem/grpc-gateway) annotations — never hand-written HTTP handlers.
- Proto files include both `google.api.http` annotations and `openapiv2` options so Swagger docs are generated alongside the gateway.
- Generated code lives in `gen/go/` (committed) and is never edited by hand.

**Workflow:**
```bash
buf generate          # regenerate gen/go/ from proto/
buf lint              # lint proto files
buf breaking --against .git#branch=main   # check for breaking changes
```

### CLI — `<project>ctl`

Every service ships a CLI binary named `<project>ctl` (e.g. `authctl`, `orderctl`).

- Built with [Cobra](https://github.com/spf13/cobra) for command structure.
- All configuration (flags, env vars, config files) is managed by [Viper](https://github.com/spf13/viper).
- Each REST/gRPC endpoint has a matching CLI command under the appropriate subcommand group.
- Viper is initialized in the root `PersistentPreRunE`; individual commands never call `viper.Get*` in `init()`.
- Config file search path: `$HOME/.<project>ctl.yaml`, `./<project>ctl.yaml`, env vars prefixed `<PROJECT>_`.

```
<project>ctl
  serve          ← start gRPC + REST gateway server
  <resource>
    create       ← maps to CreateResource RPC
    get          ← maps to GetResource RPC
    list         ← maps to ListResources RPC
    delete       ← maps to DeleteResource RPC
```

### Code Quality (Go)

- Interfaces are defined at the point of use (in `usecase/`), not at the point of implementation.
- Constructor functions (`New*`) accept interfaces, never concrete types.
- Errors are wrapped with `fmt.Errorf("context: %w", err)` — no raw `errors.New` in `usecase/` or `infra/`.
- Table-driven tests with `t.Run` for all use case logic.
- `golangci-lint` runs on every PR; `.golangci.yaml` is the single source of lint config.

```bash
go build ./...
go test ./...
golangci-lint run
```

---

## Python — Clean Architecture

### Layer Structure

Mirror the Go layout using Python packages:

```
src/<project>/
  domain/         ← dataclasses/Pydantic models, domain exceptions
  usecase/        ← business logic, Protocol classes as ports
  infra/          ← SQLAlchemy repos, HTTP clients, external integrations
  entrypoints/    ← FastAPI routers, CLI (Typer/Click), gRPC servicers
tests/
  unit/           ← usecase + domain tests (no I/O)
  integration/    ← infra layer tests against real or containerised deps
```

**Rules:**
- `domain/` has zero third-party imports.
- `usecase/` depends only on `domain/` and `typing.Protocol` abstractions.
- `infra/` is the only layer that imports SQLAlchemy, httpx, boto3, etc.

### Toolchain

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `uv` | Virtual env creation, dependency management, lock file (`uv.lock`) |
| `pytest` | Test runner — all tests live in `tests/`, discovered automatically |
| `ruff` | Linting and formatting (replaces flake8, isort, black) |
| `mypy` | Static type checking — strict mode enabled |
| `pre-commit` | Runs ruff + mypy on commit |

```bash
uv sync                          # install all deps from uv.lock
uv run pytest                    # run full test suite
uv run pytest tests/unit/        # unit tests only
uv run pytest -k "test_name"     # single test
uv run ruff check .              # lint
uv run ruff format .             # format
uv run mypy src/                 # type check
```

### Code Quality (Python) — Google Style

- Follow the [Google Python Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html).
- All public functions, classes, and modules have Google-style docstrings.
- Type annotations on all function signatures — no `Any` without an explanatory comment.
- Prefer `dataclasses` or `pydantic.BaseModel` for value objects; no raw `dict` crossing layer boundaries.
- Tests use `pytest` fixtures, not `unittest.TestCase`. Parametrize with `@pytest.mark.parametrize`.
- `conftest.py` at the `tests/` root for shared fixtures; never put fixtures in test files.
- Integration tests are marked `@pytest.mark.integration` and excluded from default `pytest` runs via `pyproject.toml`.

```toml
# pyproject.toml
[tool.pytest.ini_options]
addopts = "-m 'not integration'"
markers = ["integration: requires external services"]
```

---

## Cross-Cutting Rules (Both Languages)

- The service boundary is the `.proto` file. Any behaviour change that affects the API contract must update the proto first.
- Secrets are never hardcoded; they are injected via environment variables or a secrets manager. Config structs are populated at startup only.
- Each new feature ships with: unit tests (use case layer) + at least one integration test (infra layer) + a matching CLI command.
